From 6c666cb7a6c48d04f5d83c98f5c8ad9babd8966e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: danilo neves cruz <cruzdanilo@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 03:23:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: settle web pages once the dom goes quiet

On web, waitForAppToSettle compared view hierarchies read back to back, ten times at most with 200 ms between reads. A read that is slow enough hides how weak that check is, but two fast reads can land inside the same animation, so a tap aims at a menu that is still moving. Wait in the page instead until it has gone 50 ms and two rendered frames without dom mutations, scrolling or running finite animations, counting from the moment each animation finishes, up to 2 s or the command's timeout, then read the hierarchy once. The page stays watched while it is read, and if anything changed by then, the positions just read may already be stale, so the wait starts over: a layout that only catches up after a quiet spell, like a sheet resizing to content measured on a timer, no longer gets tapped where it was. Counting frames keeps a busy renderer from passing as quiet before it has laid out the last change, so layout-driven moves like a sheet resizing to its content still settle. Cross-origin iframes that are rendered in the viewport get the same wait inside each frame, through the driver, and the page waits again if one of them moved or navigated. The frame id attribute chromedriver sets on a frame it switches into does not count as a change. Hidden and offscreen frames are left out: they render no frames, so their wait could only run out, and nothing in them can be tapped. When the wait runs out, return a fresh hierarchy, or null while the page is still scrolling so a tap right after a scroll re-aims at a stable position. Scripts may now return promises, awaited in the same evaluation, and a value that fails to serialize or a rejection comes back as a script error. A caller that waits on the page marks its script as awaited, so a document that goes away under it fails the evaluation as a navigation instead of running the script again in the next document: it already ran and may have changed the page. A settle also tells the driver not to retry it, so the wait gets nothing back and starts over with the time it has left, while a Flutter scroll keeps the driver retry after a navigation. A window that closes under an awaited script ends it there instead of running it again in the window that takes over. A smooth scroll waits for its promise while the page is visible, so the next command starts where the scroll ended, and stops waiting once the page is hidden, before or during the scroll, since that promise only settles once the page shows again. A document that is going away does not count as hidden, so a scroll lost to a navigation still fails as one.
